call": ["is-active", "executing on thread"], "thread": ["is-active", "running A"]}}}, {"caption": "A sends a request through a non-blocking suspending client. A suspends; its saved continuation retains the state needed to resume.", "set": {"nodes": {"call": ["is-warn", "suspended; state retained"], "remote": ["is-active", "working on request"], "thread": ["", "released for other work"]}}, "packets": [{"edge": "request", "label": "request", "tone": "fwd", "kind": "page"}]}, {"caption": "The remote operation is still in progress. The released thread now runs unrelated task B. A has not completed or moved into B.", "set": {"nodes": {"thread": ["is-active", "running unrelated B"]}}}, {"caption": "The response becomes available and A becomes runnable. In this illustration B yields before A resumes on the same thread; another configuration may resume A on another eligible thread.", "set": {"nodes": {"remote": ["is-done", "response ready"], "call": ["is-active", "continues with result"], "thread": ["is-active", "running A again"]}}, "packets": [{"edge": "reply", "label": "response", "tone": "back", "kind": "page"}]}, {"caption": "A finishes. Non-blocking suspension changed thread occupancy during the wait; it did not make the remote work finish sooner or remove retained request state.", "set": {"nodes": {"call": ["is-done", "finished"], "thread": ["", "available"]}}}]}}}</script>
</section>

The arrow between the client thread and task A means “executes when runnable”; it does not mean that the thread owns A's lifetime. A coroutine's parent job supplies its structured lifetime. This scene holds the remote work constant to isolate the difference between blocking a thread and suspending a task.

[Sources: Kotlin's coroutine and thread distinction](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coroutines-basics.html), [structured child completion](https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.coroutines/kotlinx-coroutines-core/kotlinx.coroutines/coroutine-scope.html).

<!-- @after Android Example :: guide -->
### Match execution policy to the actual operation

A suspending network client can already release its caller's thread. A blocking client needs an appropriate worker dispatcher; adding `suspend` around it does not supply that dispatcher. CPU computation needs CPU execution time even inside a coroutine. Keep dispatch policy at the layer that knows the work, and make it injectable where tests need control.

[Source: Android's main-safe suspend-function guidance](https://developer.android.com/kotlin/coroutines/coroutines-best-practices).

<!-- @after Reliability Considerations :: staff -->
### Cancellation is a request the work must observe

Rethrow `CancellationException` when catching broad exceptions around coroutine work. For a long CPU loop, check cancellation at suitable intervals. For a blocking dependency, use its supported cancellation or interruption contract; an outer timeout cannot forcibly stop arbitrary code. The owner, the cancellation signal and the operation's response to that signal are three separate parts of the design.

[Source: cooperative cancellation and ensureActive](https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.coroutines/kotlinx-coroutines-core/kotlinx.coroutines/ensure-active.html).
